Scanning every `Book` per query doesn't scale, so the catalog keeps an **index**: a map from search key to matching books, built once and updated on writes.

```text
class Catalog:
    private booksByIsbn: Map<String, Book> = {}
    private titleIndex: Map<String, List<Book>> = {}
    private authorIndex: Map<String, List<Book>> = {}
    private subjectIndex: Map<String, List<Book>> = {}

    method addBook(book: Book):
        booksByIsbn[book.isbn] = book
        titleIndex.putList(book.title.toLower(), book)
        authorIndex.putList(book.author.toLower(), book)
        subjectIndex.putList(book.subject.toLower(), book)

    method searchByTitle(query: String): List<Book>:
        return titleIndex.getOrDefault(query.toLower(), [])

    method searchByAuthor(query: String): List<Book>:
        return authorIndex.getOrDefault(query.toLower(), [])

    method searchBySubject(query: String): List<Book>:
        return subjectIndex.getOrDefault(query.toLower(), [])
```

Fine math is a **rule**, not a fact about a loan — keeping it on `Lending` as a method (not hardcoded elsewhere) means one place to change the rate.

```text
class Lending:
    // ...fields from previous card
    private static finePerDay: Money = Money(1)

    method calculateFine(): Money:
        endDate = returnDate ?? today()   // still out: fine as of today
        lateDays = max(0, daysBetween(dueDate, endDate))
        return finePerDay.times(lateDays)

    method isOverdue(): boolean:
        return returnDate == null and today() > dueDate
```

Computing against `today()` when `returnDate` is null lets a member check their running fine before they've even returned the book.
